{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cf99","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e5a-32bc-7443-a1f1-5c57fcb17017"},"sequence":1575,"recorded_at":1780531400003145,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d4680851-7ee4-46f3-b9ae-940cf14558fc","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d4680851-7ee4-46f3-b9ae-940cf14558fc","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d4680851-7ee4-46f3-b9ae-940cf14558fc"},"call_id":"call_01a06e63957e76618645070df9c5f960","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-66-1\",\n  \"command\": \"head -n 60 LOGBOOK.md; echo \\\"...\\\"; grep -n \\\"TASK-260830-2xdt8t\\\\|3m2mw8\\\" LOGBOOK.md | head\",\n  \"description\": \"Check logbook format\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"# Flight Logbook\\n\\n> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\\n> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\\n\\n## 2026-09-03\\n\\n### 1632 — The generalisation was written down one round earlier and still not executed: a list is a forced traversal, prose is a memory\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/cliresult/guards_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/guard_inventory_test.go`, one production fix in `internal/cliresult/client.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-7 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §1.6, §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §17.2.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the `ErrUnregisteredExitStatus` gate in `cliresult.Read` was driven at four sampled values — `{1, 42, -1, 255}` — while its twin `axerror.decodeExitStatus`, which has the same shape and the same domain, was swept over 0..255 in the round-6 rework one file away. Restricting the reader gate to exactly its four covered values left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages, as did sparing 127 and 137 — the two statuses a wrapper most plausibly returns when `ax` never ran. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head with the delete-only control KILLED in the same run.\\n- FINDING: entry 1447 below states the exact generalisation this round violated — \\\"when a class of 'proven at one sample' is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named\\\". It was written and then not executed. The orchestrator asked for the enumerated list, accepted a sweep without it on the reasoning that the table was a means rather than an end, and the omission it would have made visible cost a whole review round. A generalisation recorded as prose is a memory; a list is a traversal. Working from memory closes the guard you were told about.\\n- FIX: `TestTheRefusalGuardInventoryIsDerivedFromTheReaderSource` parses every refusal site out of `internal/cliresult/client.go` with `go/ast` and requires a bijection with a published README table — twelve rows, each naming its function, sentinel, refusal marker, domain-evidence class from a closed vocabulary, and the test declarations that measure it. A guard added to the reader with no row is a red; a row whose marker no longer resolves is a red. A second table enumerates the guards this leaf added or reordered outside that file, `decodeExitStatus` required in it by name, because the twin one file away is exactly what a single-file table would have missed again.\\n- FINDING: the traversal immediately produced three more instances of the class, none of which any reviewer had named. The `ErrForeignDocument` branch was covered by one schema identifier, so restricting it to that identifier and routing every other foreign schema into `readSuccess` survived the suite. The \\\"schema member is not a string\\\" guard was covered by the number `5`, so restricting it to JSON numbers survived. Both are the round-6 shape: not an admission bypass, a change in WHICH FACT the machine client is told. The presence guard for a missing `schema` member survives an arbitrary predicate keyed on the exit status, which is the class round 4 declined to block on and is recorded as a stated bound rather than fixed.\\n- FIX (production, the only behaviour change this round): `encoding/json` admits JSON `null` into a `string` and yields `\\\"\\\"`, so a document whose `schema` member was `null` had been answered as a FOREIGN CONTRACT carrying the schema `\\\"\\\"` — a claim that some other contract owns the document, when what is true is that this one is not readable. Found only because the type guard's domain was swept rather than sampled. The guard now checks the raw token alongside the unmarshal.\\n- DECISION: the round-6 reviewer asked whether `exitStatusIsNotEnough` should distinguish \\\"this status carries no registered code\\\" from \\\"this status is not in the table\\\", since it computes `len(fanIn[status])` with no membership check and would report a map miss as a measured 0. The answer is not a defensive branch: a branch no production path can take promises nothing and is never measured. `TestTheFanInSentenceCannotReportAMapMissAsAMeasurement` proves the fabrication unreachable instead — `documentSchema` has exactly one caller and it is `Read`, the gate precedes that call in `Read`'s body by AST position, and every (registered version, registered failure status) pair measures at least one code, so a 0 there could only ever have been a miss. Moving the gate behind the discriminator reddens it.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, disclosed rather than fixed): the §1.6 common-data-model gate does NOT enforce the number half of that section. `Read` admits a Structured Error whose `details` carry `1.5`, which SPEC.md forbids as integers inside the IEEE 754 safe-integer range. The gate's doc comment enumerates what it owns honestly; its opening sentence and README's matching phrasing overstated it. §1.6's number rule is bound to `internal/scalar` and left unevidenced there, so nothing false is claimed in the traceability projection. The admission is now ASSERTED by a test, so closing the gap reddens the pin and forces the disclosure to be updated in the same change — a disclosure with no pin decays into a paragraph nobody re-reads.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, correction to the record): three mutants were dismissed in round 5 on the grounds that \\\"none is a registered code\\\". `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` IS registered at Structured Error 1.3.0 exit 6 (`catalog_gen.go:541`), and sparing it in `ExitCodeFor`'s version scoping survives on a clean tree. The conclusion holds for a different reason — the same narrowing on `observation_gap` is killed by `TestExitCodeForRefusesVersionAndCodeDrift`, so the class has a covered representative. The dismissal stands; the sentence behind it did not. A survivor dismissed with a wrong reason is worse than one dismissed with none, because the reason is what the next round trusts.\\n- DECISION: reviewer observation O3 was closed by measurement rather than by the stated bound the reviewer offered. `verifyEnvelopeIdentity`'s major comparison was proven at major 2 alone; it is now swept over majors 0..64 through `Read` with the bound stated for the tail. Where a domain can be swept cheaply, a stated bound is a bound about effort, and this leaf has published the rule that a bound must be about the contract.\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All eight new tests register against the existing `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the registration was probed by renaming a declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 63 to 86 mutants — 85 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 64 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green after every batch. Supersedes the \\\"63 mutants at 62 killed\\\" figure in the 1447 entry below. One mutant on the §1.6 disclosure was DISCARDED as equivalent and replaced: disabling the assertion in the test changes no outcome while the gap is open, so the replacement closes the gap in production and requires the pin to redden. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n\\n### 1447 — The exit status a document declares is a member, not a fact: a guard proven on one code let a relabelled envelope forge a retry claim\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/axerror/agreement_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/relabel_test.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-6 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — every round-6 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the code-to-exit-status agreement in `axerror.decodeBody`, `if exitCode != expectedExit`, was covered by exactly one row of `TestDecodeRefusesClosedShapeViolations`: `observation_gap` driven at exit 5 instead of its own exit 9. One code, one wrong status, out of 752 ordered (code, wrong-status) pairs at 1.0.0 and 1,744 at 1.3.0. Narrowing the guard to `code == \\\"observation_gap\\\"` — restricting it to exactly the pair that row drives — left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages; so did sparing `policy_refused` or `authentication_failed` individually. Only deleting the guard reddened anything. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written.\\n- FINDING: this was a reachable bypass and not only an unmeasured ratio. `code` and `exit_code` are two independent members of one document, and this guard is the sole thing binding them. The retryability refusal this package publishes keys on the EXIT STATUS for three whole classes — 7 authorization, 16 policy refusal, 130 interrupt — so relabelling a code out of its own class asks that refusal about the wrong class and it stays silent. Under the narrowing, `{\\\"code\\\":\\\"authentication_failed\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":9,\\\"retryable\\\":true}` was ADMITTED by `cliresult.Read` at exit status 9, and the caller received a `Reading` naming an authorization failure with `Retryable() == true`. The §14.2 exit-status corroboration cannot see it, because the process really did exit with the relabelled status.\\n- FINDING: this is the FOURTH guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231 and the command-tag agreement in entry 1421. Each round closed the guards it was shown and did not sweep the sibling on the same reading path. The generalisation, stated once: when a class of \\\"proven at one sample\\\" is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named.\\n- FIX: `TestTheCodeToExitStatusAgreementIsMeasuredOverTheWholeCodeRegistry` drives all 316 registered codes across the 4 registered versions at all 17 registered failure statuses — 5,372 rows. The agreeing status must be ADMITTED and each of the other sixteen refused with this guard's own sentence naming both numbers, so a pair settled by `decodeExitStatus`, by the closed member set, or by the retryability gate does not count as coverage of it. Per-version totals are pinned at 752, 1,056, 1,504 and 1,744 and re-derived from `CodesFor` and `CodesByExitStatus`.\\n- DECISION: close the bypass at the production entry point as well as at the decoder, because they are different claims. `TestRelabellingACodeOutOfItsExitClassCannotForgeARetryClaimThroughRead` takes the 7 codes of Structured Error 1.0.0 whose own class carries an exit-keyed retryability refusal, relabels each into all 14 statuses that carry none, and requires `Read` to refuse all 98. The control in the other direction is required in the same loop — the same code at its own status with `retryable: true` must be refused by the retryability gate — which is what makes the relabelling a bypass of something rather than a rewrite of nothing.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, closed in the same pass): the registered-status admission in `decodeExitStatus` was driven at `{0, 1, 18, 99}`, so `!IsFailureExitStatus(status) && status != 42` survived the whole suite. The complement of a four-value sample is not a bound. `TestTheExitStatusAdmissionIsSweptOverEveryByteValue` sweeps 0..255 plus six out-of-range values and requires admission if and only if the §15.2 table registers the status as a failure.\\n- DECISION: an oracle must not be the predicate under test. The sweep above enumerates the expected admissions through `ExitStatusMeaning`, not `IsFailureExitStatus`, because the latter IS the decision being measured and an oracle built from it would agree with whatever the mutant narrowed it to. The asserted row count of seventeen is what catches a mutation that moves both. The sweep also drives a code the registry does not carry, which takes the §15.3 unknown-code branch and skips the agreement guard, so exactly one decision is left in the row.\\n- ANOMALY: the two guards closed here are on the same function as gates this leaf has swept three times, and neither appeared in any of the 14-, 23-, 28-, 44- or 53-mutant harnesses. The harness grew by whatever the last review named. A hand-picked mutant set measures the reviewer's attention, not the code — the same shape entry 2113 records for a sweep that selects its own mutants.\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All three new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; each registration was probed by renaming its declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 53 to 63 mutants — 62 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 44 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the \\\"53 mutants at 52 killed\\\" figure in the 1421 entry below. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-33sfxc revision 6, handed to review.\\n\\n### 1421 — A guard proven at one ordered pair admits a forged authority document, and the same blind spot sits in the sibling reader\\n\\n- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (one subtest rewritten into a both-directions sweep, one new grid test), `internal/cliresult/datamodel_test.go` and `internal/axerror/datamodel_test.go` (large-document rows), `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-5 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §1.6. No production code changed — every round-5 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the command-tag agreement in `readSuccess`, `if result.Command() != command`, was covered by exactly one row: invoked `doctor`, document reporting `list`. Narrowing it to `result.Command() != CommandTakeover` — admit any document CLAIMING to be a takeover result — left the whole repository suite at exit 0, all 13 packages `ok`. With that in place `Read(CommandList, <a document carrying \\\"command\\\": \\\"takeover\\\">)` hands the caller a `Reading` whose `Result()` is a takeover body, the one body in this contract carrying adoption and authority semantics, for an invocation that never ran it. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written; a control sparing `CommandDoctor` was KILLED in the same run, so the one existing row was real and was all there was.\\n- FINDING: this is the THIRD guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231. The two neighbouring subtests in the SAME test function already sweep their whole domain and assert the sweep count. The pattern was in the file, two screens up, and was not applied to the row between them.\\n- FIX: the agreement is driven over the whole implemented vocabulary in both directions — every implemented tag as the invocation against every other implemented tag's own emitted document — and each refusal must carry this guard's own sentence naming both tags, so a pair settled by the version binding does not count as coverage of it. The 18 agreeing pairs are required to be ADMITTED in the same loop, which is what stops a guard that refuses everything from passing the sweep.\\n- DECISION: state the guard's share of the vocabulary as a ratio with its denominator rather than as a bound in prose. `TestTheCommandAgreementGuardOwnsAMeasuredShareOfTheTagVocabulary` drives all 1,936 ordered pairs over the 44 registered tags and classifies every answer: 18 admitted, 306 refused by this guard, 1,144 refused before stdout is read because the invoked tag selects a version this repository does not build, 468 refused by the version binding because the document claims a tag bound to another major. \\\"The guard is never reached for a pair involving an unimplemented tag\\\" is then a measured fact with a named earlier refusal behind it, not an assumption that nothing was bypassed.\\n- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, extended and acted on rather than recorded as a bound): the reviewer measured that narrowing `axerror.requireCommonDataModel` to skip documents over 4096 bytes SURVIVES, because every duplicate-member fixture is hand-built and short, and declined to block because byte length is not a §1.6 dimension. The identical narrowing on `documentSchema`'s copy of the same gate in `cliresult` also survived — the finding was reported for one reader and holds in both. Both are closed with a conforming document several times that size: a Structured Error padded through its 64-key `details` bound, and a CLI Result carrying twelve Session Summaries.\\n- DECISION: give a size fixture ONE knob. The first padding helper padded through both the 4096-character `message` bound and the `details` map, so narrowing either loop alone left the document over 4096 and the mutant survived as an equivalent one. Padding through `details` alone makes the narrowed loop redden the row's own length assertion. A fixture whose size has two independent sources cannot be shown to fail closed.\\n- ANOMALY: `Read` refuses the forged-tag pairs from three different places depending on which side is unimplemented, and only one of them is this guard. Counting refusals rather than classifying them would have reported 1,918 refusals and 306 of them would have been invisible; the classification is what turns \\\"everything is refused\\\" into \\\"this guard refuses exactly these\\\".\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. Both new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the two registrations were probed by renaming each declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 44 to 53 mutants — 52 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 36 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the \\\"44 mutants at 43 killed\\\" figure in the 1338 entry below. Same stated bound as that entry: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-33sfxc revision 5, handed to review.\\n\\n### 1338 — A figure published beside a measurement must be derived from it, not restated next to it: third instance in one artifact\\n\\n- SCOPE: `README.md` (one figure corrected), new `internal/traceability/readme_figures_pin_test.go`, `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps), `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-4 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2. No production code changed — every round-4 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `README.md` published \\\"73 executable acceptance cases\\\" while `ownership.v0.5.0.json` held 74 entries with 74 distinct ids and `tracecheck` printed `acceptance_cases=74`. The first pass of this leaf moved the registry from 65 to 73 and updated the sentence; the round-2 rework added a case and did not. Nothing measured the figure — setting it to 999 left `go test ./...` and `tracecheck` both at exit 0.\\n- FINDING: this is the THIRD occurrence of the identical class inside this one artifact, and the first two are recorded here by the same author. Entry 1015: the \\\"nineteen §15.2 rows\\\" claim against an eighteen-row table. Entry 1157: two of four published fan-in rows wrong, root cause named at the time as \\\"prose nobody measured\\\". The response to the second was `TestREADMEFanInTableIsDerivedFromTheMeasuredProjection`, which parses the README and re-derives every cell — and it sits two hundred lines from a sentence that restates instead of derives and is off by one. The tool for the class existed, in the repository, written for this exact failure, and was never pointed at the neighbouring paragraph.\\n- DECISION: the general rule, worth stating once. A figure published beside a measurement must be an INPUT to the test, not a second copy of the answer. Three instances in one artifact is not carelessness; it is the natural failure mode of prose sitting near data. The only fix that holds is parsing the prose and re-deriving every number in it from the thing it describes.\\n- FIX: `TestREADMEOwnershipFiguresAreDerivedFromTheMeasuredReport` locates the paragraph by its heading, refuses a heading it finds twice or not at all, and re-derives all seven figures from `traceability.VerifyRepository` — not only the one that drifted, because the other six were correct today and unmeasured for exactly the same reason. Each figure is located by the noun phrase naming what it counts, so re-ordering the clause cannot re-point a pin at a different number.\\n- DECISION: the pin polices its own completeness. Every digit run in the paragraph that no pinned phrase consumed is reported as unmeasured, with only dotted version and path tokens exempt under a deliberately narrow rule. That makes DELETING a row from the pinned-figure list a red rather than a quietly smaller comparison — the failure mode entry 2113 records for a derived gate that did not police its own extraction pattern.\\n- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, acted on): `exitStatusIsNotEnough` documents its own count as \\\"a measured count rather than as advice\\\" and `README.md` repeated the claim, but every assertion on those refusals matched the trailing clause `identifies no failure` and nothing read the number. Replacing `len(fanIn[exitStatus])` with a constant, or the whole `%d registered` clause with the word \\\"many\\\", left the suite green. Both are killed now, and so is the narrowing that keeps the count correct at exit 6 only — which is exactly the single assertion proposed as the minimal fix. The sweep drives every registered command at every registered §15.2 failure status through `Read` and re-derives each answer from `CodesByExitStatus`.\\n...\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 22383,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 5596,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
